WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:06.318
This is Make Your First Beats, by Monobox. Drum machines and rhythm, one step at a time.

00:00:07.118 --> 00:00:09.969
Today: your first beat and rhythms by genre.

00:00:11.769 --> 00:00:15.343
Your first beat. Building a pattern, layer by layer.

00:00:16.143 --> 00:00:28.163
The best way to build a beat is one layer at a time, listening after each change. Here is that process with the most common beat in popular music: hats in eighth notes, a backbeat snare and a simple kick.

00:00:29.663 --> 00:00:31.313
Step 1: the pulse.

00:00:32.113 --> 00:00:37.441
Start with the hi-hat on every other step. This keeps time and gives you something to build on.

00:00:38.241 --> 00:00:40.295
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:00:41.795 --> 00:00:43.560
Step 2: the backbeat.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.553
Add the snare on beats 2 and 4, steps 5 and 13.

00:00:49.353 --> 00:00:51.407
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:00:52.907 --> 00:00:54.542
Step 3: the kick.

00:00:55.342 --> 00:01:01.810
Put the kick on beats 1 and 3, steps 1 and 9. Now you have a complete, simple beat.

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:04.664
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:01:06.164 --> 00:01:07.936
Step 4: make it move.

00:01:08.736 --> 00:01:14.669
Small changes to the kick give the beat character. Add a kick on the and of 3, step 11:

00:01:15.469 --> 00:01:17.523
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:01:18.323 --> 00:01:24.334
Try other positions too: step 8 or step 16 lead into the next beat and push the groove forward.

00:01:25.834 --> 00:01:26.719
Less is more.

00:01:27.519 --> 00:01:38.518
Beginners often fill every step. Space is part of the rhythm: the silence between hits is what lets each one land. If a beat feels cluttered, remove things before you add more.

00:01:39.318 --> 00:01:47.424
Tip: Change one thing at a time, and let the loop play a few times before you judge it. Your ears need a few repetitions to hear a groove.

00:01:48.924 --> 00:01:49.665
Practice.

00:01:50.465 --> 00:01:55.968
First: program the four grids above, one after another, at about 100 BPM.

00:01:56.768 --> 00:02:04.352
Second: try the extra kick on steps 4, 8, 11, 14 and 16. Which do you like best?

00:02:05.152 --> 00:02:10.528
Third: make the hats play every step for a busier feel, then go back to every other step.

00:02:11.328 --> 00:02:15.859
Fourth: make a version with as few hits as possible that still feels like a beat.

00:02:17.659 --> 00:02:21.101
Rhythms by genre. The patterns behind the styles.

00:02:21.901 --> 00:02:36.620
Every genre has its own rhythm and its own tempo. The grids below are simple, typical starting points, not rules. Producers change them all the time. The tempos are common ranges, and individual tracks often go outside them.

00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:38.698
House.

00:02:39.498 --> 00:02:50.472
Around 115 to 130 BPM. The kick plays on every beat, “four on the floor”, the clap marks 2 and 4, and an open hi-hat on the offbeats gives the bounce.

00:02:51.272 --> 00:02:53.326
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:02:54.826 --> 00:02:55.443
Techno.

00:02:56.243 --> 00:03:06.758
Around 120 to 125 BPM. Also four on the floor, often more driving and hypnotic, with sixteenth-note hats and less emphasis on the backbeat.

00:03:07.558 --> 00:03:09.612
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:03:11.112 --> 00:03:12.562
Hip-hop (boom bap).

00:03:13.362 --> 00:03:24.088
Around 85 to 95 BPM. A heavy kick, a hard snare on 2 and 4, and usually some swing (see the next chapter). One common variant:

00:03:24.888 --> 00:03:26.942
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:03:28.442 --> 00:03:29.645
Trap and half-time.

00:03:30.445 --> 00:03:47.314
Trap beats use a half-time feel: the snare or clap lands on beat 3 instead of 2 and 4, so the beat feels half as fast as the tempo. The hi-hats run in fast sixteenths, with quick rolls of even shorter notes, and long, low 808 kicks carry the bass line.

00:03:48.114 --> 00:03:50.168
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:03:51.668 --> 00:03:52.306
Dubstep.

00:03:53.106 --> 00:04:00.048
Around 140 BPM, with a half-time feel at about 70: a kick on 1 and a big snare on 3.

00:04:00.848 --> 00:04:02.902
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:04:04.402 --> 00:04:05.454
Drum and bass.

00:04:06.254 --> 00:04:15.255
Around 174 BPM. A classic “two-step” pattern: kick on 1 and on the and of 3, snare on 2 and 4.

00:04:16.055 --> 00:04:18.109
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:04:19.609 --> 00:04:21.110
Reggaeton (dembow).

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:32.499
The dembow rhythm has a kick on every beat and a snare in a “boom, ch-boom, chick” figure, on the a of 1, the and of 2, the a of 3 and the and of 4.

00:04:33.299 --> 00:04:35.353
The example is written out in the ebook.

00:04:36.153 --> 00:04:45.787
Tip: Learn a genre by listening first. Pick a track you like, find the kick and the snare, and program what you hear. Then compare it with the grids above.

00:04:47.287 --> 00:04:48.028
Practice.

00:04:48.828 --> 00:04:54.749
First: program each grid at the tempo given, and listen to how the same three sounds change character.

00:04:55.549 --> 00:05:04.677
Second: take the house beat and slow it to 90 BPM. Then speed the boom bap beat up to 125 BPM. What happens?

00:05:05.477 --> 00:05:10.940
Third: choose one genre and make three variations of its beat by moving one or two kicks.

00:05:12.740 --> 00:05:13.832
That's all for today.

00:05:14.632 --> 00:05:15.857
If you want to keep going:

00:05:16.657 --> 00:05:25.589
Monobox is the app behind the ebook. It is a drum machine and groovebox: a place to make beats, step by step, and turn them into grooves.

00:05:26.389 --> 00:05:29.231
Everything the ebook teaches is meant to be tried with it:

00:05:30.031 --> 00:05:32.005
programming patterns on a step grid.

00:05:32.805 --> 00:05:34.937
knowing what each drum sound does.

00:05:35.737 --> 00:05:38.047
building the classic rhythms of each genre.

00:05:38.847 --> 00:05:41.737
adding swing, dynamics and sound design.

00:05:42.537 --> 00:05:44.470
turning a pattern into a whole track.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:49.801
This ebook is free. Share it with anyone who wants to make their first beats.

00:05:50.601 --> 00:05:54.575
This episode of Make Your First Beats was narrated with a synthetic voice.

